// The shape catalogue — the checks.
//
// The axis this owns is **dynamic dispatch**. It is the smallest program in the set on purpose:
// nothing else in the set needs `&Trait` or `*Trait` at all, and a feature with no guide program is
// a feature nobody is checking. What it does need is for the dispatch to be genuinely dynamic, so
// every section here drives at least two implementations through one call site, and the answers are
// far enough apart that reaching the wrong one could not produce the right number.
//
// The geometry is all closed form or hand-computable: a circle of radius 2, a 3-by-4 rectangle, a
// 3-4-5 triangle, and a pentagon whose area comes out of the shoelace formula. The composite
// figures are sums and unions of those. Nothing is compared against a value this program produced,
// except where the thing being checked *is* a law — scaling multiplies area by the square of the
// factor and perimeter by the factor — and there the law is asserted against every implementation
// in the catalogue rather than against a number.
//
// What writing it turned up:
//
//   * **THE finding: erasure forgot every trait but the one erased to — answered.** A `Circle`
//     implements `Shape` and it implements `Display`, and once it was a `&Shape` it could no longer
//     be printed. That was not a gap in the implementation, it was what the type said — an object
//     offers the trait's members and nothing else — but it meant the most ordinary thing a
//     heterogeneous collection does, print itself, could not be written. The static half of the
//     language never had the problem: a `[T: Shape + Display]` bound has both, because a bound is a
//     list and a type is one name. So **the price of erasing is paid in capabilities, not just in
//     speed**, and it is charged at the moment a program stops knowing the type — which is the
//     moment it most needs to describe it.
//     What answers it is `trait Shape: Display`: a **required** trait is a promise the trait makes,
//     so its members are in the object's table alongside the trait's own, and `print(s)` on a
//     `&Shape` is one indirect call. Two customers wanted this feature and they wanted different
//     things — `guide/sha2` wanted an economy, one bound instead of nine; here it was the
//     difference between a thing being writable and not, and it is the one that decided the table
//     layout. What the object still cannot do is become a `&Display`: the slots are there and no
//     word names them as a table of their own, which is the price of flattening.
//   * **A bound did not see the object's table — answered.** This program reported a second, sharper
//     charge on the same price: `show[T: Display](x: T)` would not take a `&Shape`, so a required
//     trait was reachable by *name* and not by *constraint*, and no one generic function covered
//     both a `Rect` and the `&Shape` holding it. A bound now asks what may be **called** on the
//     value, and an object's table is exactly the members it names — so `show(s)` compiles, and a
//     `[T: Shape]` function serves a `Circle` with a direct call and a `&Shape` with an indirect
//     one, from one signature. Nothing about monomorphization moved to allow it: an object type is a
//     concrete type, so the body is instantiated at `&Shape` the way it is at `Circle`. The
//     conversion is what stays refused, and the distinction is the whole of the rule — being callable
//     is not being assemblable, and only the first of the two is what a bound asks about.
//   * **A `*Writer` rendered bytes and nothing turned them back into a string — answered.** The
//     natural way to write the trait's text member is not to build a string at all — it is
//     `render(self, out: *Writer)`, which is what `Display` already is and what costs no
//     allocation. It could not be used, because after rendering into a `ByteSink` there was no way
//     to get a `string` out of the bytes. So both routes to text were blocked at once, and the
//     program concatenated strings. `from_utf8` answered one and the requirement answered the other:
//     the trait member is `render`, `str(s)` on an erased shape goes through the table, and the
//     `describe(s: &Shape)` this program used to need is gone. It also removed a quadratic: `Group`
//     used to build a fresh string per part.
//   * **A fixed-point number cannot be rendered into a sink.** `six(x)` is `f"$x%.6f"` and still
//     allocates, because the sink family renders a real as `%g` — significant digits. `14 §2`
//     decided that the conversion letter does not cross into a `Display`, only a width, a precision
//     and a justification, so there is nowhere to ask for `%f`. That is the one allocation left on
//     this program's rendering path, and it is a recorded decision rather than a gap.
//   * **There is no way to ask a trait object what it forgot.** No cast, no test, no pattern — `g
//     is Circle` is a syntax error, because a type is not a pattern. A catalogue that wants to
//     count its circles has to be told, so `Shape` declares a `kind` property and every impl
//     answers with a constant that duplicates, by hand, exactly the fact the vtable pointer
//     already is. This looks like a deliberate omission rather than a gap — a downcast is the one
//     operation that makes erasure a lie, and Rust needs a whole `Any` machinery to offer it — but
//     it should be written down as a decision, because the workaround is a maintenance burden that
//     grows with the number of shapes.
//   * **A `Buf` is indexable, and iterable only through its view — on purpose.** `cat[i]` reads
//     through the prelude's `Index`, which is why the catalogue is walked by subscript here. There
//     is an iteration protocol now (`Iterate`), and `Buf` deliberately does not implement it:
//     `cat.view()` is a slice over storage that is already there, so walking it costs an index
//     where a cursor would cost a call per element. The protocol is for sequences whose elements
//     have to be *computed*, which is what a container's elements never are. So both halves this
//     program reported are closed, and the second one is closed by a decision rather than by code.
//   * **Object safety is enforced exactly as `02` describes, and the diagnostics are the best in
//     the compiler.** All three rules were probed. A `Self` away from the receiver, an associated
//     function, and a `&self` method under `*Trait` are each refused with a message that says what
//     the rule is and, for the last one, which sigil to write instead. Worth recording as a
//     positive: this is what the rest of the error surface should read like.
//   * **A property is object-safe and reaching it through a table needed nothing.** `kind` is a
//     member with no parameter list, and reading `s.kind` on an erased value goes through the
//     object's table exactly as a call does — which is what `02` claims and had no guide program
//     checking.
//   * Positives worth keeping: a combinator holding `&Shape` makes recursion through the object's
//     table ordinary, so a group of groups needs nothing special and neither does a `render`
//     that calls itself down the tree; a `Buf[&Shape]` is a heterogeneous growable collection with
//     no annotation anywhere; the two sigils cost nothing to mix, with `*Shape` over stack values
//     allocating nothing at all; and an object built and dropped a hundred thousand times in a
//     loop neither leaks nor double-frees, including when its payload holds objects of its own.

import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf, ByteSink}
import sysl.math.Float

check(name: string, got: string, want: string)
    if got == want then print(s"ok    $name = $got")
    else print(s"FAIL  $name = $got, wanted $want")
end check

section(title: string) = print("-- " + title)

// The two numbers a shape is asked for, to six decimals — once, over a **bound**, for every way of
// holding one.
//
// This used to be two functions, `size(s: &Shape)` and `size_raw(s: *Shape)`, with identical bodies.
// A `&Shape` is still not accepted where a `*Shape` is wanted, and there is still no spelling for
// that crossing: for a plain reference it is `&*x` — a dereference is a place and the address of a
// place is a raw pointer, which is the greppable discipline `03` wants for the unsafe tier — but an
// object has no dereference, so `&*o` is "a &Shape has forgotten what it points at".
//
// What removed the duplication is not a conversion, it is that a bound takes an object. Both sigils
// carry a table of `Shape`'s members, which is what `[T: Shape]` asks for, so one body serves both —
// and serves a bare `Circle` as well, with a direct call instead of an indirect one. An observer per
// sigil was never the only way to write this; a bound was, once it could see the table.
size[T: Shape](s: T) -> string = six(s.area()) + " " + six(s.perimeter())

// A bound, to six decimals.
extent(b: Box) -> string =
    six(b.x0) + " " + six(b.y0) + " " + six(b.x1) + " " + six(b.y1)

close(x: f64, y: f64) -> bool = (x - y).abs() < 0.000001

pentagon() -> &Buf[Point]
    var p: &Buf[Point] = buf()

    p.push(Point(0.0, 0.0))
    p.push(Point(4.0, 0.0))
    p.push(Point(5.0, 3.0))
    p.push(Point(2.0, 5.0))
    p.push(Point(-1.0, 3.0))
    p
end pentagon

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

section("each shape answers for itself")

var circle = Circle(0.0, 0.0, 2.0)
var rect = Rect(1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0)
var tri = Tri(Point(0.0, 0.0), Point(4.0, 0.0), Point(0.0, 3.0))
var poly = Poly(pentagon())

// The bound takes the value itself here, so these four are direct calls into four monomorphized
// bodies — no erasure, no table, nothing to forget.
check("a circle of radius two", size(circle), "12.566371 12.566371")
check("and the box around it", extent(circle.bounds()), "-2.000000 -2.000000 2.000000 2.000000")
check("a three-by-four rectangle", size(rect), "12.000000 14.000000")
check("and the box around it", extent(rect.bounds()), "1.000000 2.000000 4.000000 6.000000")
check("a three-four-five triangle", size(tri), "6.000000 12.000000")
check("and the box around it", extent(tri.bounds()), "0.000000 0.000000 4.000000 3.000000")
check("a five-sided polygon", size(poly), "21.000000 17.535658")
check("and the box around it", extent(poly.bounds()), "-1.000000 0.000000 5.000000 5.000000")

// …and the same body, reached with the shape erased behind each sigil in turn. One signature, three
// ways of holding the argument, one answer.
var as_raw: *Shape = &circle
var as_counted: &Shape = Circle(0.0, 0.0, 2.0)

check("the same function through a raw object", size(as_raw), "12.566371 12.566371")
check("and through a counted one", size(as_counted), "12.566371 12.566371")

// The two spans a box answers with, which is what a layout asks one for rather than its corners.
check("a circle's box is as wide as it is tall",
      str(close(circle.bounds().width(), 4.0) && close(circle.bounds().height(), 4.0)), "true")
check("and a rectangle's box has the rectangle's own two sides",
      str(close(rect.bounds().width(), 3.0) && close(rect.bounds().height(), 4.0)), "true")

// The triangle's vertices the other way round enclose the same area — the shoelace sign is which
// way the outline was traced, and a shape has no opinion about that.
var flipped = Tri(Point(0.0, 3.0), Point(4.0, 0.0), Point(0.0, 0.0))

check("a triangle traced the other way", six(flipped.area()), "6.000000")

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

section("one call site, many implementations")

// One variable, four types, four answers, all read through the same expression — so the call
// cannot have been resolved when it was compiled.
var it: *Shape = &circle
var seen = ""

seen += six(it.area())
it = &rect
seen += " " + six(it.area())
it = &tri
seen += " " + six(it.area())
it = &poly
seen += " " + six(it.area())

check("one variable reaching four implementations", seen,
      "12.566371 12.000000 6.000000 21.000000")

var four: [4]*Shape = [&circle, &rect, &tri, &poly]
var total = 0.0

for s in four do total += s.area()

check("four types of different sizes in one array", six(total), "51.566371")

var edges = 0.0

for s in four do edges += s.perimeter()

check("and their perimeters", six(edges), "56.102028")

// A property read through the table, which `02` says is as safe to erase as a method.
var kinds = ""

for s in four
    if kinds != "" then kinds += " "

    kinds += s.kind.name()

check("a property reached through the object", kinds, "circle rect triangle polygon")

// Counting by kind is the workaround for a downcast that does not exist.
var circles = 0

for s in four
    circles += s.kind match
        Circular -> 1
        _ -> 0

check("counting the circles the long way", str(circles), "1")

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

section("a shape made of shapes")

var parts: &Buf[&Shape] = buf()

parts.push(Circle(0.0, 0.0, 2.0))
parts.push(Rect(1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0))
parts.push(Tri(Point(0.0, 0.0), Point(4.0, 0.0), Point(0.0, 3.0)))

var group: &Shape = Group(parts)

check("a group of three", size(group), "30.566371 38.566371")
check("its box is the union of theirs", extent(group.bounds()),
      "-2.000000 -2.000000 4.000000 6.000000")
check("and it says what it is", group.kind.name(), "group")

// A group holding a group — the recursion goes through the object's table, and nothing in `Group`
// knows that one of its parts is another one.
var outer: &Buf[&Shape] = buf()

outer.push(group)
outer.push(Poly(pentagon()))

var nested: &Shape = Group(outer)

check("a group of a group", size(nested), "51.566371 56.102028")
check("its box reaches around both", extent(nested.bounds()),
      "-2.000000 -2.000000 5.000000 6.000000")

// Rendering it walks the same tree the same way, and `str` on the object reaches the `display`
// the requirement put in its table.
check("and describing it walks the tree", str(nested),
      "group(group(circle(r=2.000000), rect(3.000000x4.000000), triangle), polygon(5))")

// A group of one is that shape; a group is not obliged to be interesting.
var lone: &Buf[&Shape] = buf()

lone.push(Circle(0.0, 0.0, 2.0))

var single: &Shape = Group(lone)

check("a group of one is what it holds", size(single), "12.566371 12.566371")

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

section("a shape that wraps a shape")

var moved: &Shape = Moved(group, 10.0, 20.0)

check("moving changes neither number", size(moved), "30.566371 38.566371")
check("but it moves the box", extent(moved.bounds()),
      "8.000000 18.000000 14.000000 26.000000")

var scaled: &Shape = Scaled(group, 2.0)

check("doubling quadruples the area", size(scaled), "122.265482 77.132741")
check("and doubles the box", extent(scaled.bounds()),
      "-4.000000 -4.000000 8.000000 12.000000")

// Wrappers compose, because a wrapper is a shape and a wrapper takes a shape.
var both: &Shape = Scaled(Moved(group, 10.0, 20.0), 2.0)

check("a wrapper wrapping a wrapper", size(both), "122.265482 77.132741")
check("and the box it works out to", extent(both.bounds()),
      "16.000000 36.000000 28.000000 52.000000")
check("which says what it is made of", str(both),
      "scaled(2.000000, moved(group(circle(r=2.000000), rect(3.000000x4.000000), triangle)))")

// Scaling by one is the shape it wrapped.
var unchanged: &Shape = Scaled(group, 1.0)

check("scaling by one changes nothing", size(unchanged), "30.566371 38.566371")

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

section("the law every shape obeys")

// The catalogue, one of each — including the three combinators, which is the point: a law checked
// through a trait object is checked against every implementation there is, and a wrapper is an
// implementation like any other.
var all: &Buf[&Shape] = buf()

all.push(Circle(0.0, 0.0, 2.0))
all.push(Rect(1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0))
all.push(Tri(Point(0.0, 0.0), Point(4.0, 0.0), Point(0.0, 3.0)))
all.push(Poly(pentagon()))
all.push(group)
all.push(Moved(group, 3.0, -1.0))
all.push(Scaled(group, 0.5))

check("seven implementations in the catalogue", str(all.len()), "7")

for s in all.view()
    var name = s.kind.name()
    var big = Scaled(s, 3.0)

    check("scaling " + name + " by three multiplies its area by nine",
          str(close(big.area(), s.area() * 9.0)), "true")
    check("and its perimeter by three",
          str(close(big.perimeter(), s.perimeter() * 3.0)), "true")

for s in all.view()
    var name = s.kind.name()
    var shifted = Moved(s, 7.0, -4.0)

    check("moving " + name + " leaves its area alone",
          str(close(shifted.area(), s.area())), "true")
    check("and its perimeter alone",
          str(close(shifted.perimeter(), s.perimeter())), "true")
    check("and slides its box by exactly that much",
          str(close(shifted.bounds().x0, s.bounds().x0 + 7.0) &&
              close(shifted.bounds().y1, s.bounds().y1 - 4.0)), "true")

// A shape is inside its own bound, and a bound is not smaller than the shape's own width.
for s in all.view()
    check("the box around " + s.kind.name() + " contains the shape's own box",
          str(s.bounds().within(s.bounds())), "true")

// A group of the whole catalogue has the area of the whole catalogue.
var everything: &Shape = Group(all)
var summed = 0.0

for s in all.view() do summed += s.area()

check("a group of them all sums them all", str(close(everything.area(), summed)), "true")

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

section("counted and raw")

// The same shape reached through both sigils. A raw object points straight at a value, so nothing
// is allocated to form one; a counted object owns the box it erased.
var stack = Rect(0.0, 0.0, 6.0, 7.0)
var raw: *Shape = &stack
var counted: &Shape = Rect(0.0, 0.0, 6.0, 7.0)

check("a raw object over a value on the stack", six(raw.area()), "42.000000")
check("a counted object over a box", six(counted.area()), "42.000000")
check("and they agree about everything else", str(raw.perimeter() == counted.perimeter()), "true")

// A shape built where a counted object is expected is boxed and erased in one step, and two arms
// of different concrete types meet at the one type.
var pick = 1
var chosen: &Shape = pick match
    1 -> Circle(0.0, 0.0, 2.0)
    _ -> Rect(1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0)

check("two arms of different types meet at one object", six(chosen.area()), "12.566371")
check("and it is the circle that was chosen", chosen.kind.name(), "circle")

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

section("what an object keeps and what it forgets")

// A shape that is still a `Circle` prints, because `Display` is reached on the type.
var printable = Circle(0.0, 0.0, 2.0)

check("a shape prints while its type is known", f"${printable}", "circle(r=2.000000)")
check("and pads like anything else does", f"${printable}%22s", "    circle(r=2.000000)")

// Erased, it prints too, and that is what the requirement bought: `display` is a slot in the
// object's table beside `area` and `kind`, so nothing about the call site changes when the type
// goes away. The specifier crosses it intact — `14 §2` says the field belongs to the whole value,
// and the implementation on the far side of the table is the one that honours it.
check("erased, it prints exactly the same", f"${chosen}", "circle(r=2.000000)")
check("and pads exactly the same", f"${chosen}%22s", "    circle(r=2.000000)")
check("str reaches it through the table", str(chosen), "circle(r=2.000000)")

// What it does *not* keep is its identity. The kind property is the hand-written copy of what the
// object's first word already is, and it is still the only way to ask.
check("but it answers only what the trait asked", chosen.kind.name(), "circle")

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

section("objects made and dropped")

// A counted object holds one count of the box it erased, so the ordinary discipline has to carry
// over unchanged. A long loop is what turns a leak into a growing heap and a double free into a
// crash, rather than into a test that passes.
var spun = 0.0
var i = 0

while i < 100000
    var s: &Shape = Circle(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)

    spun += s.area()
    i++

check("a hundred thousand objects made and let go", six(spun), "314159.265359")

// The payload holds objects of its own, so releasing the outer one has to release what it held —
// the box's destructor doing its work through a pointer whose type has been forgotten.
var deep = 0.0

i = 0

while i < 20000
    var inner: &Buf[&Shape] = buf()

    inner.push(Circle(0.0, 0.0, 1.0))
    inner.push(Rect(0.0, 0.0, 2.0, 2.0))

    var g: &Shape = Scaled(Group(inner), 2.0)

    deep += g.area()
    i++

check("and twenty thousand holding objects of their own", six(deep), "571327.412287")

// A catalogue that grows reallocates its storage, which moves every object in it.
var grown: &Buf[&Shape] = buf()

i = 0

while i < 5000
    grown.push(Circle(0.0, 0.0, 1.0))
    i++

var after = 0.0

for s in grown.view() do after += s.area()

check("five thousand objects through a buffer that grew", six(after), "15707.963268")
check("and the buffer still says how many", str(grown.len()), "5000")
